# Every change to this repo must be approved by a human. The ruleset on the
# default branch requires code-owner review, and the single rule below makes
# gp-contrib the owner of every path.
#
# delegate[bot] is a GitHub App and cannot be a team member, so a code-owner
# requirement naming a team is structurally unsatisfiable by it. That is what
# lets delegate review this repo: a bot approval is a signal to humans, never
# sufficient to merge.
#
# gp-contrib is used because it is the only team with write access to this
# repo; gp-secops has read only, and GitHub silently ignores code owners
# without write access.
#
# If this is ever narrowed from `*` to a list of paths, the list must cover at
# least: the bot and its review gates (all of /delegate/, not just
# pr-reviewer.ts, since the framework, worker, and subagent prompts all shape
# its behaviour); AWS permissions (/deploy/components/ci-roles*,
# /deploy/components/identity-center*, /deploy/deploy.sh); account-level IaC
# (/deploy-org/, /deploy-workbench/); all of /.github/workflows/, since the
# OIDC trust policies key on the ref, not the workflow file, so a newly added
# workflow on main can assume the deploy roles and one on any branch can
# assume the PR preview role; and this file. Scope each by
# directory rather than by file: a narrower gate leaves the same hole one file
# over.

*    @thegoodparty/gp-contrib
